{
  "image_name": "0cbbf17bcf66aee5ee480acd0eee3179.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how antiangiogenic agents can reshape a tumor’s blood vessels with either helpful or harmful consequences for cancer immunity. On the beneficial side, treatment appears to “normalize” abnormal tumor vasculature, shown by more organized vessels associated with pericytes. This normalization relieves hypoxia in the tumor microenvironment and reduces immunosuppression, allowing more blue immune effector cells to enter the tumor. A curved feedback loop suggests an immunostimulatory vascular-modulating cycle, involving IFNγ, in which improved immune infiltration and vascular remodeling reinforce each other. This also seems to improve the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in a more immunostimulatory tumor microenvironment.\n\nIn contrast, the detrimental side shows that excessive vessel pruning can leave tumors with sparse, poorly functional blood supply and a hypoxic core. The labeled consequences include reduced drug penetration and increased hypoxia. Under these conditions, yellow immunosuppressive cells infiltrate more strongly, shifting the microenvironment in a less favorable direction. Overall, the image emphasizes that antiangiogenic therapy has a dual role: in the right balance it can support immune attack by normalizing vessels, but if overdone it may worsen hypoxia and promote immune suppression.",
